      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
