    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351167",
      "knowledge_point_id": "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨4{t}^{3},\\,-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{t}^{2},\\,-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨12(1)^{2},\\,-4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
