    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121920",
      "knowledge_point_id": "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{2}^{t-1},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t-1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{t-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨{2}^{1-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2(1)}{({1}^{2}+1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{2}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{2}{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡2+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4{ln}^{2}⁡2+1}}{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
