    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121919",
      "knowledge_point_id": "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),1-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+1},-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨\\frac{3}{3(2)+1},-2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
