    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351176",
      "knowledge_point_id": "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩,$ measured in $\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨3{t}^{2}+2t-1,-4t+5⟩.\n$$\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the acceleration is\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t+2,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6(1)+2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|a(1)|:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨8,-4⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the acceleration at time $t=1\\,\\text{s}$ is $4\\sqrt{5}{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
