    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351377",
      "knowledge_point_id": "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector",
      "problem": "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-8t,\\,8{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-8,\\,24{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
