      "math_academy_id": "q-7165",
      "knowledge_point_id": "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5},\\,3{t}^{2}-1⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4},\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3},\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
