      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+2,\\,5+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5+{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-8t,\\,3{t}^{2}-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-6t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-8,\\,6t-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨6(1)-8,\\,6(1)-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
