      "math_academy_id": "q-351396",
      "knowledge_point_id": "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5,\\,6+{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(3)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6+{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t,\\,3{t}^{2}-8t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-8t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-4,\\,6t-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(3) & =⟨6(3)-4,\\,6(3)-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14,\\,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
