    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-121932",
      "knowledge_point_id": "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector",
      "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨4-2{t}^{2},1-3{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-3{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,-9{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-9{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨-4,-18(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,-18⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
