    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7224",
      "knowledge_point_id": "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm s,$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t^4-t^2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFind the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3}-2(1),6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(1)|\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨2,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
